#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac9e7d-ea92-5cd3-af26-4c902196e80b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Line Integrals of Unity"}] :question/id #uuid "55e8e57f-7f9e-529a-aa1b-24e1d86c4133" :question/math-academy-id "e-9470" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/238460fd6d89a120c21c88a681a7f2e3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4$. Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\nL = πr = 4π\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e60b3dd1-0842-562d-a72f-5adb6a60cba8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Line Integrals Over Piecewise-Smooth Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "fdab3229-66ca-59d3-ac20-8a5b3d3506fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-9471" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/bb0d714825f714ebc1e0612dd9fc507e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/2dab4e2a2bea7b9217543ff7144f9dce.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n$\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s = \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s$.\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n$\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L$.\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96d48adf-a293-54ae-88fd-ee8471e5fc65" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Independence of Parametrization and Orientation"}] :question/id #uuid "4d5a8f9d-7324-547c-ae5e-b49294debc1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9472" :question/problem "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}C_{1}:r_{1}(t)|= ⟨\\frac{1}{9}t^{3}, t⟩, & 0 \\le t \\le 3 \\\\ C_{2}:r_{2}(u)|= ⟨3u^{3}, 3u⟩, & 0 \\le u \\le 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k$. Which of the following must be true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n$\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n$x = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3$.\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n$\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e60b3dd1-0842-562d-a72f-5adb6a60cba8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Line Integrals Over Piecewise-Smooth Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d70edc-380b-545e-ab46-11f583e47a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(π + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163557} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd51827-049f-59bf-b639-3d6cd4188086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(π + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163558} {:answer/id #uuid "13c8e868-d9a5-57e6-af50-6481b2e61437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(π + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163559} {:answer/id #uuid "344e3e08-79d8-5f3a-8686-79643c3bd884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(π + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163560} {:answer/id #uuid "6403a8c3-5306-5334-9692-941cbfe9be7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2π + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5d70edc-380b-545e-ab46-11f583e47a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(π + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163557} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1372f5f-d206-5ac9-9ea1-38ac16bb0dca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163556}] :question/id #uuid "ef2f2e1e-2df6-5eea-a523-f7500a9f7301" :question/math-academy-id "q-122481" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/q-122481.png)\n\nCalculate $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $2$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac9e7d-ea92-5cd3-af26-4c902196e80b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Line Integrals of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9737c065-3808-503d-899e-2a6d66c494f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163581} {:answer/id #uuid "a2986e57-3f8c-50eb-a62f-c4a83b6c35c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163582} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0f2e1d-67d6-5888-aef0-1d6b5a83fc00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163583} {:answer/id #uuid "720d7173-610d-564f-904b-abb4dea86d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163584} {:answer/id #uuid "768df7fb-4b22-53e1-be89-5208c75e6864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "720d7173-610d-564f-904b-abb4dea86d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163584} :answer-field/id #uuid "acb37489-1257-581d-90dd-f8d303f1ae6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163580}] :question/id #uuid "418cd3d5-ecfd-5690-a4c6-98dd1a3d29e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122768" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/q-122768.png)\n\nCalculate $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the line segment shown above."}]}
